Subject: Materialises the per-function register property cache at frame setup and drops both inline-cache guards

What this does
  Materialises the per-function register property cache at frame setup and drops both inline-cache guards.

Why we need it
  Every inline named-property read and write in the register backend opened with `site != REG_IC_NONE && f->pic != NULL`, but the encoder assigns a real site to every `s`-format opcode, so on the hit path the site test is always true; and the table's lazy allocation ran *inside* the fill, i.e. Only after the guard had already chosen the miss path, so the guard bytes were pure overhead on the hot path.

What it adds
  `js_regvm_execute` materialises the table at `restart:` before the opcode loop, and `regvm_t_call_record`/`regvm_t_call_method_record` do the same before `regvm_record_alloc`, because a threaded callee entered through a record never runs `restart:`; both throw on OOM. With the table present whenever `regvm_pic_count != 0` the guards are removed from `regvm_t_get_local_field_0`, the `T_GET_FIELD_BODY` macro, `regvm_t_put_field`, the monolith get_local_field/get_field sites and the fused re-dispatch sites at `quickjs.c:29917`/`29942`; the cold `regvm_t_put_field_slow` tail keeps its test.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
